theory.intro:
The **Cartesian equation** of a path links $x$ and $y$ with no $t$ in it. Make $t$ the subject of one component and substitute it into the other. The values $t$ can take restrict which part of the curve is traced.

The figure shows $x=t$ and $y=t^{2}$ for $t\ge0$. The path is $y=x^{2}$ for $x\ge0$: the right-hand half is traced, and the left-hand half is not traced.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.0,0)--(3.2,0) node[right,font=\footnotesize] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.2) node[above,font=\footnotesize] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,gray,domain=-1.9:0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.9,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x});
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (1.9,3.61) {traced};
\node[above left,font=\footnotesize] at (-1.9,3.61) {not traced};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The component equations give
$\begin{aligned}\cos t&=\dfrac{x+2}{3}\\\sin t&=\dfrac{y-3}{3}.\end{aligned}$
Use the identity $\cos^{2}t+\sin^{2}t=1$:
$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\dfrac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}&=1\\(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}&=9.\end{aligned}$
For $0\le t\le\pi$, $\sin t\ge0$, so $y\ge3$. The path is the upper semicircle with centre $(-2,3)$ and radius $3$, traced anticlockwise from $(1,3)$ to $(-5,3)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(3,0);
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(-3,0);
\draw[black,thick] (3,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=3];
\draw[black,->] (1.5,2.598) arc[start angle=60,end angle=80,radius=3];
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.4pt);
\fill[black] (3,0) circle (1.4pt);
\fill[black] (-3,0) circle (1.4pt);
\path (0,0) node[below] {$(-2,3)$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$3$};
\path (3,0) node[below right] {start $(1,3)$};
\path (-3,0) node[below left] {end $(-5,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
